        "id": "ch17_2_1-1",
        "text": "Printer server settings\nClicking the Server item on the Printer Configuration window menu bar produces\nthe Basic Server Settings window, shown in Figure 17-9.\nYou can play with a few different settings to help with your printer\nadministration:\n\u00bb\n\u00bb  Publish Shared Printers Connected to This System: Allows remote clients\nto connect to any of the local printers marked as shared (more on that later).\nFIGURE 17-9:\nThe Printer\nConfiguration\nBasic Server\nSettings window.\n334   PART 4 Junior Administrator Boot Camp\n\u00bb\n\u00bb  Allow Remote Administration: Enables remote clients to connect to the\nCUPS server running on this system.\n\u00bb\n\u00bb  Allow Users to Cancel Any Job (Not Just Their Own): By default, normal\nusers can only cancel their own print jobs. Enabling this feature allows any\nuser to cancel any other user\u2019s print job. Although this is a handy feature, it\ncan get dangerous in a huge multiuser environment (especially if your users\nlike playing tricks on one another).\n\u00bb\n\u00bb  Save Debugging Information for Troubleshooting: By default, the CUPS\nserver generates a moderate amount of logging information to monitor\nusage or problems. If you\u2019re having trouble with a specific printer configura-\ntion, you can enable this feature to produce more (lots more) information in\nthe log files.\nBesides these options additional advanced are options available by clicking the\nAdvanced Server Settings drop-down button. The advanced options allow you to\ncontrol how CUPS manages the print job logs. After determining the settings\nappropriate for your CUPS server environment, you can move on to creating the\nindividual local printers.",
